#
# WHY 300 AND NOT THE 120s DEFAULT
#
# Measured 2026-08-14 on this machine, unloaded: the full test-cmd
# (`for t in staging/plugin/scripts/tests/*.test.sh; do bash "$t" || exit 1; done`)
# runs 79 harnesses in 159s, all green. The default ceiling of 120s is therefore 39s SHORT of
# the suite's own runtime — the gate could never finish, so every arming turn spent 120s and
# ended in a timeout, verifying nothing. 300 gives roughly 2x headroom over the measured value.
#
# Slowest four, same measurement, for whoever re-tunes this after adding harnesses:
#   worktree-isolation-contract  30.4s
#   spec-coverage                 9.0s
#   fence-contract-coverage       9.0s
#   stop-gate-path-predicate      8.0s
# The remaining 75 are each under 8s and the tail is flat — there is no outlier to split out
# into a separate fast lane.
#
# This is a snapshot of its moment, not a standing fact: adding harnesses moves it. Re-derive
# from the files rather than trusting this block (CLAUDE.md rule 13).
#
# NOT the same number as CI. The `shell-tests` job runs this suite AND `plant-check.sh`, and the
# whole job took ~25 min on 2026-08-14 — that duration is plant-check's, not the suite's, and
# attributing it to the suite is what made this gate look unrecoverable.
#
# 2026-08-19 — RE-DERIVED, 300 -> 600 (CLAUDE.md rule 14: recorded forward, the block above is left
# exactly as it was written on its own day). ADR-0156 flagged this file as measured against 79
# harnesses when there were 85; measured now, same machine, same test-cmd: **415s over 85 harnesses**,
# all green. So 300 had fallen BELOW the suite's own runtime — the identical defect ADR-0137 §D4
# fixed at 120s, reappeared by growth rather than by an error, which is why the block above says to
# re-derive rather than trust it.
#
# WHY 600 AND NOT 850. 2x the measured value (the headroom ratio 159 -> 300 used) would be ~830, and
# the convention argued for it. 600 is the operator's call, taken 2026-08-19: 1.45x headroom, and a
# suite that hangs blocks a turn for 10 minutes instead of 14. The stated risk is that 415s was
# measured on an unloaded machine — under concurrent agent builds (the contention ADR-0159 §D1 just
# addressed) it grows, and 1.45x is a thinner margin than this file has previously carried. If a
# timeout reappears here, that is the first thing to re-measure, not a reason to raise blindly.
#
# ADR-0161 changes what a high ceiling COSTS, which is why raising it at all is affordable: the
# fingerprint cache means an unchanged tree does not re-run the suite, so the ceiling is paid once
# per real change instead of on every turn spent waiting on a dispatch.
#
# Ceiling: stop-gate.sh caps any value at TMO_MAX=900 and falls back to 120 with a stated reason
# on a non-positive-integer value. See ADR-0137 §D4.
